    slug: 'debloat-vs-factory-reset',
    title: 'Debloat vs. Factory Reset: Which Actually Improves Android Performance?',
    excerpt: 'Data-driven comparison: debloating reclaims 1.5–3GB RAM and 20–30% battery without data loss, while factory reset requires full re-setup. When to use each.',
    date: '2026-11-01',
    category: 'Comparisons',
    readTime: '6 min',
    tags: ['Factory Reset', 'Performance', 'Battery', 'RAM'],
    content: `
## Debloat vs. Factory Reset

Both methods aim to speed up your Android phone, but they work very differently. Here is the data-driven comparison.

| Factor | Debloat (AI-Phone-Debloater) | Factory Reset |
|--------|------------------------------|---------------|
| Data loss | None — keeps apps, accounts, settings | Wipes everything |
| RAM reclaimed | 1.5–3GB (Tier 1–2) | Temporary, returns as apps reinstall |
| Battery gain | 20–30% | Short-term only |
| Time required | 5–15 minutes | 30–60 minutes + re-setup |
| Reversible | Yes — snapshot restore | No |
| Root required | No | No |
| Warranty impact | None (User 0 ADB) | None |

## When to Debloat

Use debloat when your phone is sluggish from pre-installed bloat but your data and setup matter. Tier 1 removes carrier junk, ad daemons, and telemetry. Safe and reversible.

## When to Factory Reset

Use factory reset when the OS itself is corrupted, settings are misconfigured beyond repair, or you are selling the device. Expect to reinstall apps and sign back in to everything.

## Recommendation

Start with **Tier 1 debloat + dry-run**. It is the lowest-risk way to reclaim RAM and battery without losing data. Keep the snapshot for instant rollback.

---

*Last updated: 2026-11-01*
    `
